{
  "@context": {
    "schema": "https://schema.org/"
  },
  "@type": "Note",
  "@id": "note:notion-253",
  "title": "Thought as Technology",
  "status": "draft",
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "b-1",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 1,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Thought as Technology"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-3",
      "type": "bulletList",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "b-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Part 1: The Missing Link in Digital Thinking"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-4",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "We are drowning in tools for managing data and documents, yet starving for tools that help us think. Spreadsheets crunch numbers. Notion organizes notes. AI summarizers regurgitate fragments. But where do we go when we want to connect, synthesize, and evolve ideas? When we want our tools not just to store knowledge, but to process it?"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-5",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "This is not a question of productivity. It's a question of cognition."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-6",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "We all love to quote McLuhan:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-7",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "> \"We shape our tools, and then they shape us.\""
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-8",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": ">"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-9",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "[VISUAL: Black card with white text. “we shape our tools—and then they shape us into PDF hoarders.” Minimal, meme-able.]"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-10",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "But lately, it feels like our tools are shaping us into people who hoard PDFs and forget why we opened them. The tools we use today are optimized not for understanding, but for storage and speed. We can save everything, access anything—but that doesn't mean we understand what we're looking at."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-11",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "To move forward, we need to map the terrain of digital cognition. What kinds of thinking do our current tools support? And where do they fall short?"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-12",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Alongside [McLuhan](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marshall_McLuhan), thinkers like [Douglas Engelbart](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Douglas_Engelbart) (Augmenting Human Intellect), [Maggie Appleton](https://maggieappleton.com/garden-history) (digital gardens), [Andy Matuschak](https://notes.andymatuschak.org/About_these_notes) (evergreen notes), and [Bret Victor](http://worrydream.com/) (humane representations) have all shaped how we imagine the future of thought tools."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-13",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "---"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-14",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "## How We Got Here: Data, Information, and the Illusion of Knowledge"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-15",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Let's start with foundational distinctions:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-16",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "### Data"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-17",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "**Data** is raw input: numbers, fragments, signals."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-18",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "### Information"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-19",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "**Information** is structured data—organized, categorized, visualized."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-20",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "### Knowledge"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-21",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "**Knowledge** is contextualized understanding—meaning situated in purpose, relevance, and belief."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-22",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "With these terms in hand, we can define three categories of tools:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-23",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "[VISUAL: Simple 3-tier pyramid or nested circles labeled \"Data → Information → Knowledge\" — clean, monochrome, no decoration.]"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-24",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "*These categories aren’t just semantic—they shape how we build, and how we think.*"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-25",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "### 1. Data-Processing Tools"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-26",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **Examples:** Excel, Google Sheets, SQL"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-27",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **Purpose:** Computation, analysis"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-28",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **Strength:** Precision with structured input"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-29",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **Limitations:** Cannot manage context or meaning"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-30",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "### 2. Knowledge-Storage Tools"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-31",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **Examples:** Notion, Obsidian, Roam"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-32",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **Purpose:** Organization, reference, documentation"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-33",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **Strength:** Contextual memory, linking"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-34",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **Limitations:** Passive, user-driven; no synthesis or feedback"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-35",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "### 3. Knowledge-Processing Tools (emerging)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-36",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **Purpose:** Evolving, synthesizing, applying understanding"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-37",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **Potential:** Bridge information and insight"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-38",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **Status:** Still speculative, rarely realized"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-39",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "[VISUAL: 2x2 grid. X-axis: Storage → Synthesis. Y-axis: Data-focus → Knowledge-focus. Place Excel, Notion, Obsidian, etc., in their respective quadrants.]"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-40",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "---"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-41",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "## Where Ideas Go to Stall"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-42",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "While we have ample tools for raw data and static knowledge, we lack tools that support the in-between: tools that help ideas move, evolve, and interconnect. Tools that not only hold our thoughts, but shape them."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-43",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "To build such tools, we must go beyond storage. We need systems that reflect, challenge, and guide our reasoning. That identify gaps in our models and help us revise them. That don't just remember what we thought yesterday, but help us think better today."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-44",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "This is not merely a UX challenge. It is an epistemic one. A cognitive one. A philosophical one."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-45",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "If tools are extensions of the mind, what kind of mind are we extending?"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-46",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "And it is the frontier we now stand before."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-48",
      "type": "bulletList",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "b-47",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Part 2: What Would a Knowledge Processing Tool Actually Do?"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-49",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Knowing isn’t the hard part. Thinking is."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-50",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A knowledge processor isn’t just a place to remember. It’s a space that helps you rethink."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-51",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "If Part 1 defined the gap, Part 2 begins to explore the bridge."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-52",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A knowledge processing tool wouldn’t just hold what we know. It would help us *work with* what we don’t yet understand. It would behave less like a filing cabinet and more like a collaborator—a partner in synthesis, inference, and reflection."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-53",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "## From Notes to Knowing"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-54",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Where knowledge storage tools collect, a knowledge processor connects. It would:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-55",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **Synthesize** inputs into coherent structures"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-56",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **Infer** implications, contradictions, and hypotheses"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-57",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **Evolve** with our thinking over time"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-58",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **Translate** insights into actions, models, and decisions"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-59",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "This is not just note-taking with flair. It’s structured epistemic interaction."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-60",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "## The Core Capacities of a Knowledge Processor"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-61",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "| Capability | Description |"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-62",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "| --- | --- |"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-63",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "| Contextual Recall | Understands not just what you know, but why it mattered when you saved it |"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-64",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "| Semantic Linking | Surfaces meaningful relationships beyond explicit backlinks |"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-65",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "| Insight Generation | Suggests contradictions, analogies, and patterns across ideas |"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-66",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "| Temporal Layering | Tracks how ideas evolve, diverge, or cohere over time |"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-67",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "| Decision Support | Helps simulate consequences and weigh alternatives based on prior knowledge |"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-68",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "| Modular Abstraction | Lets you compress complex thinking into reusable cognitive components |"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-69",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "| Meta-Cognition Support | Surfaces blind spots, cognitive biases, and epistemic assumptions |"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-70",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "## This Is What It Should Feel Like"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-71",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Imagine this:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-72",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- You write a new idea. The system surfaces three older notes it meaningfully contradicts—and asks you to reconcile them."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-73",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- A model you built two months ago has become unstable. The tool points out where new assumptions are creeping in."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-74",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- You outline a decision. The tool overlays related past decisions, outcomes, and forgotten factors you once considered important."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-75",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "It is not predicting your thoughts. It is holding a mirror to your reasoning."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-76",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "## From Second Brain to Epistemic Partner"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-77",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The \"second brain\" metaphor helped externalize memory. But knowledge processing demands more. It requires a kind of *epistemic companionship*."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-78",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- One that doesn’t just recall, but reflects."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-79",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- One that doesn’t just store, but surfaces."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-80",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- One that doesn’t just retrieve, but refines."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-81",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Such a system wouldn’t automate understanding. It would *amplify* it. The goal isn’t speed. It’s clarity."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-82",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A true knowledge processor is not a better notebook. It is a new kind of thinking environment—alive with feedback, context, and challenge."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-83",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The next leap in computing won’t be faster processors. It’ll be better questions."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-85",
      "type": "bulletList",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "b-84",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Part 3: Toward Epistemic Amplification"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-86",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "---"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-87",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "What are we really trying to amplify?"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-88",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Not memory."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-89",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Not efficiency."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-90",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Not even knowledge, in the static sense."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-91",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "It’s thinking itself."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-92",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The goal of knowledge tools shouldn’t be to do more faster. It should be to understand more clearly. To refine judgment. To expose blind spots. To stretch the quality and coherence of thought."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-93",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "## Thinking With the Tool, Not Just in It"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-94",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Most digital tools are containers. They hold. They display. They store."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-95",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "But a true knowledge processor would act more like a **lens** and a **mirror**:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-96",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- A **lens** that reveals patterns across contexts, disciplines, and time"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-97",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- A **mirror** that reflects assumptions, contradictions, and epistemic gaps"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-98",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "This shift is subtle but profound. It changes the purpose of the tool from documentation to cognition."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-99",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "## Epistemic Scaffolds: Designing for Cognitive Growth"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-100",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "To support actual thinking, we need structures that engage us in it. Call them **epistemic scaffolds**—design patterns that promote reflection, synthesis, and revision."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-101",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "These might include:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-102",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **Modules**: Idea-units that can be reused, extended, and abstracted"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-103",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **Mirrors**: Timelines or views that show how concepts evolve"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-104",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **Lenses**: Filters that surface themes across unrelated ideas"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-105",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **Simulations**: Lightweight environments for testing beliefs and models"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-106",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "These are not productivity features. They’re cognitive environments."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-107",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "## Friction Is a Feature"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-108",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Modern UX worships frictionlessness. But shallow smoothness can be the enemy of deep thought."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-109",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Good friction interrupts automaticity:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-110",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- A prompt asking, *“What does this assumption depend on?”*"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-111",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- A timeline that reveals, *“You haven’t revisited this belief in two years.”*"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-112",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- A contradiction detector that highlights, *“This conflicts with something you wrote last week.”*"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-113",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Friction like this doesn’t slow us down—it pulls us in."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-114",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "> “Amplification is not automation,” writes Michael Nielsen. “It’s helping humans do what they do best, at a higher level of abstraction and quality.”"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-115",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": ">"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-116",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A tool that writes for you isn’t amplifying you—it’s replacing you. True epistemic tools push you to reason more rigorously, not less."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-117",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "## The Mind, Shaped by Environment"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-118",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "What we believe, how we reason, even what we notice—all of it is shaped by the environments we inhabit."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-119",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The right tools don’t just change what we do. They change *who we become*."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-120",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "If we want to cultivate a culture of deeper, clearer, more courageous thinking, we need to build tools that reward that kind of engagement. That means:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-121",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- Designing interfaces that challenge, not just capture"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-122",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- Prioritizing synthesis over collection"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-123",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- Valuing clarity over quantity"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-124",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "> When our environments change, so does the mind that inhabits them."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-125",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": ">"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}